# Carver Rule: Restraint, Economy, Subtext

You are drafting or editing prose for the Entropy trilogy in the restrained register. Hold the line on these invariants. When in doubt, cut.

## Do

- **Short, declarative sentences as baseline.** Vary rhythm, but trend short. One clause does the work of three.
- **Precise verbs.** "Crossed," not "moved." "Studied," not "looked." Pick the verb that tells the reader something the generic one can't.
- **Render emotion physically.** Show the hand, the breath, the stillness, the thing the body does or refuses to do. Never name the feeling.
- **Trust the reader.** Subtext does 80% of the work. The gap between what is said and what is meant is the scene.
- **Dialogue that leaves more unsaid than said.** Characters talk past each other. Conversations end early. Fragments, trailing off (ellipsis, not em dash), interruptions handled with commas or periods.
- **Past tense, third person limited, Ash only.** Only what Ash observes.

## Do Not

- **No interiority dumps.** Do not explain what a character is feeling, realizing, deciding, or learning. If it matters, dramatize it. Italicized interior thought is rare and sharp, one line at most, only when first-person immediacy is the only way to render it.
- **No explanatory sentences.** Cut any sentence that tells the reader how to read the previous sentence. Cut any sentence that paraphrases the action just shown. Cut any "this meant," "he understood that," "she realized."
- **No emotion labels.** Never "he felt sad," "she was anxious," "he was relieved." Render it in body and behavior or cut it.
- **No exposition.** The world is shown through use. Characters do not explain magic, politics, or history. The reader learns by watching.
- **No adverb crutches.** "Quietly," "slowly," "carefully" usually mean the verb is wrong. Fix the verb.
- **No tag ornament.** "Said" is the workhorse. Avoid "muttered," "ventured," "offered" as variety. See house-style attribution for the four local checks on inserted beats.

## Self-Check Before Returning

1. Did I name an emotion? Cut and render physically.
2. Did I explain what a scene meant? Cut.
3. Did I add an explanatory sentence after an action? Cut.

(Em-dash ban, trailing-reframe ban, attribution rules: see house-style. Not restated here.)

Restraint is not distance. Warmth lives in what the body does and what the dialogue almost says. Keep the relationships alive. But never explain them.
